      "text": "To solve for the logarithm of $\\sqrt{3}$ with base 3, we can use the logarithmic identity $\\log_{a}(a^b) = b$.\n\n$3 \\cdot \\log_{3}(27\\sqrt{3}) = \\log_{3}(27^{2} \\cdot 3)$\n\n$\\log_{3}(27^{2} \\cdot 3) = 2\\cdot \\log_{3}(27) + \\log_{3}(3)$\n\nUsing the logarithmic properties, $3\\log_{3}(27) \\approx 3\\log_{3}(3^3) = 9$ and $\\log_{3}(3) = 1$.\n\nTherefore, $\\log_{3}(27) = \\log_{3}(3^3) = 3$.\n\nPlugging this value back in, we get $\\log_{3}(27^{2} \\cdot 3) = 2\\cdot3+1 = 7$.\n\nSo, $\\log_{3}(27\\sqrt{3}) = 3+2 = 27$.",
